title: Staal's Announces His Successor
view-if: year >= 1934 and month >= 2 and chancellor == "Staal" and staal_post and staal_successor_time == rubicon_time
on-arrival: {!
Q.staal_resign = 1;
Q.goerdeler_gnu_unlock = 0;
Q.bredow_unlock = 0;

if (Q.nationalists_staal >= 10 || Q.nationalists_staal >= 8 && Q.staal_marcher_influence < 5 && Q.staal_marcher_r < 5) Q.goerdeler_gnu_unlock = 1;
if (!Q.goerdeler_gnu_unlock) Q.bredow_unlock = 1;
!}
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
tags: event

= Staal's Successor

With his resignation announced last week, speculation over who will succeed the monumental leader now dominates the minds of citizens across the nation. Some believe he is prepared to pass power to a civilian, while others expect another military man to take the helm. In the end, it hardly matters—the Commonwealth has been changed forever.

- @goerdeler
- @bredow

@goerdeler
title: Reynold Goodlake - <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">**GNU**</span>
subtitle: Former <span style="color: #3E88B3;">nationalist</span> mayor of Leipzig. Strong conservative.
face-image: img/portraits/goerdeler.jpg
choose-if: goerdeler_gnu_unlock
on-arrival: {!
Q.chancellor = "Goodlake";
Q.gnu_leader = "Goodlake";
Q.gnu_right += 5;
Q.staal_approval -= 5;

Q.staal_marcher_r -= 5;
Q.staal_marcher_influence -= 5;
Q.staal_marcher_support -= 6; 
!}

Goodlake, an outspoken conservative on the Commonwealth Right, with personal authoritarian and monarchist leanings, represents Staal’s signal of a return to partisan rule in essence. Despite breaking with his former party to join the <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">**GNU**</span>, his appointment is seen by workers and <span style="color: #000000;">repu</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">bli</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">cans</span> as little different from naming a <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Traditionalists**</span> man.

Yet, the fact that he is not a military figure like Staal or his circle has brought some measure of relief. Though a long-time opponent of Alfred Mallin and his faction, Goodlake remains a conservative at heart and is expected to continue the dismantling of the despised Unity order.

But Goodlake is no Staal. He lacks the vast network of political ties that his patron carefully cultivated across Commonwealth society, and Staal’s personal popularity has failed to pass to his chosen successor. Moreover, Goodlake is known for his hardline fiscal conservatism and anti-<span style="color: #c00000;">social</span> stance, having once called for the abolition of the Poorman's Pension during the Goodryke era.

Perhaps the house of cards Staal so carefully built is already beginning to wobble.

@bredow
title: Ferdinand von Bredon - <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">**GNU**</span>
subtitle: Deputy Defense Minister. Ideology suspected to be similar to Staal's.
face-image: img/portraits/bredow.jpg
choose-if: bredow_unlock
on-arrival: {!
Q.chancellor = "Bredon";
Q.gnu_leader = "Bredon";
Q.staal_approval -= 4;

Q.staal_marcher_r -= 3;
Q.staal_marcher_influence -= 4;
Q.staal_marcher_support -= 5; 
!}

Presenting himself as a continuation of Staal and as another non-partisan military man, von Bredon has won his close friend Staal's favor. Although former chancellor Staal is officially retired, he still pulls many strings behind the scenes, unwilling to give up that scheming of his. 

However, von Bredon is certainly no Staal, he possesses no vast network of political connections throughout all walks of life, and Staal's personal popularity has largely failed to transfer over to his hand picked successor.

Perhaps the house of cards Staal has carefully built will come tumbling down?